menu "UI Component Configuration"

    config UI_DEEP_SLEEP_COUNTDOWN_SEC
        int "Deep sleep countdown duration (seconds)"
        default 30
        range 10 300
        help
            Duration in seconds for the countdown timer from light sleep
            to deep sleep. The timer is armed by the moon tick on the
            LIGHT-SLEEP DUSK's last frame (the fully unilluminated side
            on screen, UI_MOON_DUSK_ENABLE) -- the sleep depth follows
            the animation, never a fixed timer at the sleep entry -- and
            triggers the transition to deep sleep when it expires.

            Recommended: 30-60 seconds for battery-powered devices.

    config UI_LIGHT_SLEEP_TICK_MS
        int "LVGL tick period in light sleep (milliseconds)"
        default 100
        range 50 1000
        help
            LVGL timer tick period when in light sleep mode.
            Slower tick reduces CPU usage and power consumption.
            Default is 100ms (vs 10ms in active mode).

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_ENABLE
        bool "Play the synodic boot sweep (new moon -> now) at boot"
        default y
        help
            At boot the moon starts on a BLACK screen and replays the
            current synodic cycle -- from the last NEW MOON (a dark
            disk) to now, at most 29.5 days -- in UI_MOON_ANIM_FRAMES
            frames, baked off screen and displayed as atomic whole
            frames on the same pipeline as the burn-in spin (one ~150 ms
            frame, no top-to-bottom wipe, the display stays live
            throughout). The sweep always starts dark (the black screen
            fades into the reveal; the first frames are nearly black)
            and ends on the current phase, and hands the burn-in spin
            (UI_MOON_SPIN_*) back at the exact on-screen pose. This is
            the BOOT animation only: waking from sleep (touch, tilt or
            an alarm dismiss) shows the current moon phase STATIC, with
            no animation.

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_FRAMES
        int "Synodic boot sweep frame count"
        default 336
        range 5 400
        help
            How many phase frames make up the boot sweep, evenly spaced
            from the last NEW MOON to the current phase (the span is the
            moon's current age this cycle, at most 29.53 days). The
            frame count is fixed, so the sweep wall time is fixed
            (~frames x per-frame bake+display) while the PHASE STEP
            shrinks as the cycle is younger: at 336 (the default) the
            step is up to ~0.088 d/frame (~2.1 h) at an old moon and
            down to a few hours near first quarter. The intermediate
            frames are baked at UI_MOON_ANIM_BAKE_PX (see there); the
            final frame is always full MOON_PX. Lower = faster but
            coarser sweep; the display stays live either way.

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_BAKE_PX
        int "Synodic boot sweep intermediate-frame bake resolution (px)"
        default 240
        range 100 400
        help
            The on-screen size the boot sweep's INTERMEDIATE frames are
            baked at before being upscased (nearest-neighbor) to the full
            MOON_PX for display. 240 (the default) keeps the 336-frame
            sweep under ~3 min (~0.28 s bake + ~0.23 s display+tick per
            frame). Measured on the 336-frame sweep: 200 -> ~0.21
            s/frame (~2.4 min), 240 -> ~0.28 s (~2.8 min), 320 -> ~0.48 s
            (~4.0 min), 400 (= MOON_PX) -> ~0.72 s (~5.3 min) -- 400 bakes
            every frame at full resolution, pixel-identical to the
            static/spin bakes (no upsample softness at the terminator),
            at the cost of a ~5 min sweep. The reduced-res frames are
            physically correct (the lighting/relief model is
            resolution-independent; only the screen sampling is coarser,
            400/S x 400/S blocks), which is acceptable in the fast
            montage. The last frame and the static/spin phase are ALWAYS
            full resolution regardless of this setting. (The light-sleep
            dusk does not bake intermediate frames at all: its constant-
            projection re-renders at full resolution, and its final
            dark-side frame is a full-resolution bake.)

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_ON_WAKE
        bool "Re-bake the moon on every wake from sleep (testing)"
        default y
        help
            Obsolete: the synodic sweep (UI_MOON_ANIM_*) now plays at
            BOOT only (from a black screen); wakes show the current
            phase static. The old re-bake test hook is gone. Kept so
            existing sdkconfig files keep validating.

    config UI_MOON_DUSK_ENABLE
        bool "Light-sleep dusk: pole-axis spin to the fully unilluminated side"
        default y
        help
            When the device enters light sleep the moon (parked at the
            exact free-spin pose, seamless seam) spins about its POLE at
            the current phase -- 1 degree per frame, the shortest arc to
            the fully unilluminated view (at most a half turn, 14-95 s
            at the ~0.2 s/frame display pace) -- and the deep-sleep RTC
            countdown (UI_DEEP_SLEEP_COUNTDOWN_SEC) is armed on the
            LAST frame's display, so the sleep depth follows the
            animation. The spin is a CONSTANT PROJECTION, not a re-bake:
            the renderer's color depends only on the body-frame surface
            direction and the epoch's fixed sun (the camera basis never
            enters the color), so the fully-lit body-frame equirect map
            (864x432) is baked ONCE for the epoch (~1.5-2 s) and every
            intermediate frame re-projects that fixed map through the
            folded camera basis -- a gather-class pass (~50 ms), no
            per-frame shadow/lighting recompute, and no terminator
            flicker (a fixed surface point keeps its exact color).
            The FINAL (dark-side) frame is one full MOON_PX bake at the
            endpoint (pixel-exact lighting) and lingers on the sleep
            screens; the dark side keeps its contrast (earthshine +
            relief-lit rims, dark_zero stays 0). Waking from sleep shows
            the CURRENT phase static at the parked pose -- no
            animation; the burn-in spin resumes from exactly the pose
            the dusk started from.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_ENABLE
        bool "Free spin: rotate the moon in place (phase-anchored)"
        default y
        help
            The current phase is baked once (the static frame) and then
            rotated in-plane on screen by a FREE SPIN (a nearest-neighbor
            gather, ~6 ms/frame -- no re-bake): a spin angle integrated
            per frame whose rate is smoothed toward a target. While the
            device is in use (touched or tilted within the settle window)
            the rate is the ACTIVE period below; once it settles the rate
            coasts to the PHASE-TRUE speed -- one full turn per synodic
            moon month (29.53 days, ~12 deg/day) -- so the pose tracks
            the real moon phase. The continuous slow coast moves the lit
            pixels around the panel, preventing AMOLED burn-in (the same
            intent as the old fixed-period burn-in spin, without a fixed
            period). Picking the device up spins it faster; letting it
            settle slows it visibly back to the phase rate. The render
            runs on core 1; the display (LVGL) runs on core 0; they are
            serialized on the frame buffer so the display never tears.
            The spin runs on every screen that shows the moon and is
            paused (not stopped) while the tilt-driven orbit re-bake
            owns the buffer.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_PERIOD_S
        int "Active spin: seconds for one full turn (while in use)"
        default 180
        range 30 7200
        help
            The spin rate while the device is in use: one full in-plane
            rotation in this many seconds (the default 180 s = a turn
            every 3 minutes). After the last user activity (touch or
            tilt) the rate eases down toward the phase-true coast (one
            turn per synodic month) over the settle window. Lower = a
            faster (more visible) spin while in use; the display duty
            scales with it.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_SETTLE_S
        int "Spin settle window: seconds of idle before coasting to phase"
        default 10
        range 2 60
        help
            After this much time without user activity (touch or tilt)
            the spin rate begins easing from the active period down to
            the phase-true coast (one turn per synodic moon month). The
            rate change is exponential (time constant ~ this/3), so a
            few multiples of this window settle the spin fully. Larger
            = the moon keeps its active spin longer after use.

    config TEST_AUTO_WAKE_SEC
        int "Test: auto-wake from sleep after N seconds (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: while the device is in an idle (sleep) state,
            trigger the idle-wake path automatically after N seconds of
            being asleep -- no touch needed. Lets the wake-from-sleep
            transitions (WiFi reconnect, screen switch, moon
            re-bake/animation) be exercised repeatedly without touching
            the device. 0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_SETTINGS_DEMO_SEC
        int "Test: scripted settings run N seconds after boot (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: run the scripted settings flow N seconds after
            boot (waits out the boot sync first): settings hub ->
            pomodoro (set + save a 90-minute interval) -> alarm time
            (set + save 12:34 on all days) -> light-sleep (set the
            idle timeout + countdown + animation flags + save) ->
            airplane, logging every transition, the scheduler /
            settings-store writes, and reading the alarm / settings
            files back. Headless acceptance path for the whole
            settings stack (state machine, persistence, scheduler
            math). 0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_BAKE_NO_RELIEF
        bool "Test: bake without the relief gradient cache (perf A/B)"
        default n
        help
            Test hook: pass a NULL relief cache to every bake (relief-free
            L = clip(d, 0, 1)) to measure how much of the per-frame bake
            cost is the 2 relief-cache PSRAM loads + tangent-basis dots
            vs the common path (base direction, texture tables, albedo
            load, earthshine, tonemap). Temporary profiling aid; off by
            default and visually wrong (flat terminator).

    config UI_IMU_ENABLE
        bool "Drive the moon viewport from the QMI8658 IMU (device tilt)"
        default y
        help
            The moon's in-plane pose is the free spin's (UI_MOON_SPIN_*,
            which rotates the baked phase in place); the device tilt
            (QMI8658 gravity) changes which side of the sphere the
            camera sees (the out-of-plane orbit, see UI_MOON_ORBIT_*).
            Tilt also counts as user activity: it holds the idle timer,
            wakes the device from sleep, and drives the spin's active
            rate (picking the device up spins it faster). Touch is not
            an input to the moon's pose (it is only the core-UI wake
            input). When the IMU is absent or this is disabled the
            orbit is off (the tilt monitor runs on but gets no pose
            input, so no tilt-driven activity/wake; the free spin still
            runs when UI_MOON_SPIN_ENABLE is set).

    config UI_IMU_ACTIVITY_DEG
        int "Tilt activity threshold: departure from the settled pose (degrees)"
        default 5
        range 1 45
        help
            The monitor measures the DEPARTURE (deg) of the current
            pose from a settled-pose reference (a slow EMA that only
            updates while settled), not the delta between two raw
            samples (which amplifies per-sample noise and fired the
            "wakes right after entering sleep" bug). While the device
            is AWAKE, a departure above this threshold counts as user
            activity: it holds the idle timer, so a device operated
            purely by tilt cannot be stranded asleep. At rest the
            departure settles back below it and the reference follows,
            so a still device is free to sleep. A two-consecutive-
            sample streak gates the fire.

    config UI_IMU_WAKE_DEG
        int "Tilt wake threshold: departure from the settled pose (degrees)"
        default 20
        range 5 45
        help
            While the device is ASLEEP, a departure above this
            (deliberate-pick-up) threshold wakes it, gated by a
            two-consecutive-sample streak. Higher than
            UI_IMU_ACTIVITY_DEG on purpose: table vibration and the
            measured at-rest drift (~0.4 deg/s -> ~2.5 deg of steady
            departure) must not wake, only a deliberate tilt. Lower it
            for more sensitive wake.

    config UI_IMU_ANGLE_SIGN
        int "IMU angle sign (+1 or -1, flips the orbit direction)"
        default 1
        range -1 1
        help
            The board's IMU mounting orientation is board-specific; if
            tilting the device peeks the moon the "wrong" way, set this
            to -1. Applies to the orbit (UI_MOON_ORBIT_*) only: the
            tilt activity/wake monitor measures a DEPARTURE (an angle
            from the settled pose), so it is sign-agnostic.

    config UI_IMU_ROLL_OFFSET_DEG
        int "IMU roll offset (degrees, aligns the resting orientation)"
        default 0
        range 0 359
        help
            Obsolete: the free spin (UI_MOON_SPIN_*) is time-driven,
            not tilt-driven, so a resting roll offset has no effect.
            Kept so existing sdkconfig files keep validating.

    config UI_CLOCK_ROLL_SIGN
        int "Clock-roll correction sign (+1 or -1)"
        default 1
        range -1 1
        help
            The in-plane ROLL of the device (the angle of the gravity
            vector in the screen plane) counter-rotates the clock text +
            seconds arc (lv_obj_set_style_transform_rotation) so the
            clock stays HORIZONTAL as the device is tilted in its own
            plane (with the orbit, UI_MOON_ORBIT_ENABLE, off on the S3
            this is the tilt's only moon-UI coupling). The correction is
            the roll's departure from a settled-pose reference (updated
            only while the device is settled, so at-rest drift does not
            walk the text around), dead-zoned to 1 deg, and only applied
            when the screen plane has a gravity component (near-flat the
            roll is undefined and the last value is kept). 0 correction
            (the settled pose) costs nothing -- LVGL takes its direct
            draw path -- so the cost is only paid while the device is
            actively tilted. The board's IMU mounting orientation may
            flip the measured roll; if the text counter-rotates the
            WRONG way when the device is tilted, set this to -1.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_ENABLE
        bool "Sphere model: peek at the dark side via out-of-plane tilt"
        default n
        help
            The moon behaves like a sphere: the out-of-plane tilt of the
            device (the angle between the screen normal and vertical,
            0 deg at vertical / wall-mounted, 90 deg flat on a table)
            rotates the camera around the moon, so the dark side can be
            peeked at -- on a near-full moon this reveals the
            unilluminated limb. This requires a re-bake with a
            pre-rotated camera basis (~1.7-2.4 s with the relief DEM
            on this board); the finished frame is then displayed as one
            atomic whole frame, exactly like the burn-in spin (one
            ~150 ms frame -- the same sequential update the spin uses),
            so the viewport change lands as a frame instead of a slow
            wipe. The free spin (UI_MOON_SPIN_*) rotates the baked
            frame in place on top of the orbit's viewport.

            DISABLED by default: the ~2 s relief-DEM re-bake cannot be
            served at the tilt-driven cadence on the ESP32-S3 (the
            display pipeline stalls and the viewport lags the tilt), so
            on the S3 the tilt drives the CLOCK orientation instead
            (UI_CLOCK_ROLL_SIGN) and the moon stays on its default
            viewport. The in-plane free spin and the tilt activity/wake
            monitor are unaffected. Re-enable on the ESP32-P4, which can
            hold the re-bake rate.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_MAX_DEG
        int "Max orbit (peek) angle in degrees"
        default 30
        range 0 60
        help
            The viewport only moves by the tilt up to this limit:
            beyond it the orbit is clamped. 0 disables the orbit
            (the moon stays on its default viewport regardless of
            out-of-plane tilt). A wall-mounted device reads ~0 deg
            (vertical = the default viewport); flat on a table it
            reads 90 deg, which clamps to this limit.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_STEP_DEG
        int "Orbit quantization step in degrees (re-bake trigger)"
        default 1
        range 1 10
        help
            The orbit angle is quantized to this step and the re-bake
            only runs when the quantized target moves. 1 degree is the
            default (no visible stutter at rest); raise it (e.g. 3-5)
            if the ~2.3 s re-bake makes viewport changes feel too
            slow for the device's re-draw performance.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_DEADZONE_DEG
        int "Orbit dead zone in degrees"
        default 2
        range 0 15
        help
            Quantized orbit angles inside +/- this band map to the
            default viewport (0), so table jitter (at-rest floor
            measured at ~1.5 deg/s mean, 3.3 deg/s p90 at 1 Hz on
            this board) cannot walk the viewport around.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_LPF_ALPHA
        int "Orbit low-pass filter coefficient in percent (5-95)"
        default 30
        range 5 95
        help
            Exponential smoothing on the orbit angle, applied at the
            20 Hz UI tick, as a percentage: 30 = alpha 0.30 per tick.
            Together with the dead zone and the quantization step this
            eliminates sensor stutter; raise it for a snappier
            (jitterier) response, lower it for a heavier low-pass.

endmenu
